{"id":"F513","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"2A","direction":"against","note":"The proactive-disclosure dataset records a contract at its contract date. So the C$15.40M original (4 Jun 2021) falls in calendar 2021 and does not sit in the 2022–2025 sums behind F447/F448. Only the C$3.88M amendment could land in 2022, and its date was not shown. The point only weakens comparisons against pre-2024 baselines. It cannot explain the post-pandemic 2024→2026 declines (F508–F512, −53% to −77%). The '2.5x FY24-25 spend' ratio compares a multi-year contract with one year of spend, so it is not like-for-like. Downgraded to weak."},
{"id":"F514","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"2A","direction":"against","note":"New: it corrects the PHAC 2022 baseline in F448 to about C$6.8M, so 2022→2025 is about −51% rather than −65%, and it trims the 2024 all-federal figure by about C$1.5M. It does not touch F508/F509, which were already deduplicated. The direction of the federal decline stands; only its size shrinks."},
{"id":"F515","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"2A","direction":"against","note":"The NRC part repeats F492 (C$74K in H1'26 vs C$57K in 2025). The PHAC Apr–Jun 2026 rise (+280%) is on a tiny base (C$0.08M→C$0.30M, about 4% of PHAC's FY24-25 spend of about C$7.3M), and it coexists with deduplicated H1'26 at −53% y/y (F509). A single C$151K call-up drives most of it. Not evidence that the trough has passed."},
{"id":"F528","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"I verified the Q3'26 count of 36,312 against NCBI eutils. But GEO is almost entirely published research data, sequenced 1–3 years earlier, while the thesis tail is clinical (about 80% of remaining 6000 Gb is clinical). It also lacks an X-platform comparison: if X GSMs grew faster, the 6000 share is still falling. At most, it is a lagged signal that research 6000 output persisted through 2024–25. Downgraded to weak."},
{"id":"F531","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"Largely known: no NovaSeq X Dx exists and only the 6000Dx (2022) does. The EU IVDR framing is the only new piece. Direction 'against' is correct, because it supports a stickier tail and so cuts against the ~5 points of conversion. However, most clinical 6000 volume runs LDT or RUO workflows rather than 6000Dx kits (see F534), so a missing X Dx does not bind most of the tail."},
{"id":"F533","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"TSO Comprehensive is a NextSeq 550Dx product, which is mid-throughput and not the 6000. 'NextSeq 550Dx only' is already known. Its IVDD/IVDR status tells us nothing about NovaSeq 6000→X conversion. If it showed anything, lock-in would cut against the thesis rather than support it."},
{"id":"F534","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"New detail: the US 6000Dx intended use is targeted sequencing only and not WGS, and the page names no assays. This implies little true 6000Dx-locked volume, so less of the tail is regulation-bound and more can convert. Caveat: FDA-approved tests such as F1CDx and Caris MI Cancer Seek name the RUO NovaSeq 6000 in their PMAs, not the 6000Dx. The regulated tail therefore extends beyond 6000Dx kits, and the inference that 'regulated volume is small' is overstated."},
{"id":"F538","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"The PDF did not render, so I could not open it directly. It is corroborated independently: the UConn Jan 2025 rate for the S4-300 ($16,578, F479) exactly matches the claimed Feb 2024 Open Offer list. Correction: all the rises fall between Sep 2022 and Feb 2024, which is before or at the start of the Q2'24→Q2'26 ratio series (2.2x→3.4x). UConn's Feb 2026 price ($15,938, −3.9%) suggests the 6000 list was cut in 2025. So list moves do not explain the 2024–26 widening of the ratio. They explain the level of the gap, not its drift. NextSeq 550 rose by the same percentage, so this was a general legacy-platform increase."},
{"id":"F539","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Mostly a restatement of D029 (2.4x at GSA/NCI prices) using the Open Offer schedule. Calling the gap 'X realized 17–28% below schedule' ignores the known alternative: the 6000 mix is skewed to small SP/S2 flow cells at 4–10x per Gb (D028/D032), and Gb shipped is not the same as Gb consumed. It also uses ≥$100M as the top tier, while F540 says ≥$50M, so the two items are internally inconsistent. The 25B list of $16,000 is the 2024 figure; it was $16,480 in Jan 2025."},
{"id":"F540","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"I could not read the PDF to check the tier table. Internal arithmetic holds: 12% off $2.00 is $1.76 and 22% off $3.20 is $2.50, a drop of −29.5%. The top-tier threshold is inconsistent with F539 (≥$50M vs ≥$100M) and needs a check. The inference that 'half or a quarter' pricing must come from bespoke contracts is fair. This is schedule evidence only."},
{"id":"F543","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1B","direction":"against","note":"New check of the known 43% clause against actual catalogue prices. Caveats: it compares a 25B list price with an S4 baseline (the clause covers the highest-throughput product, which the 25B now is), and the March 2021 baseline (~$4/Gb in the known notes vs $4.80 on list here) is unconfirmed. The conclusion that the clause does not force further cuts holds either way, because the 25B is about 42–58% below on any baseline."},
{"id":"F548","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"supports","note":"New: the NextSeq 1000/2000 XLEAP list cuts were not in the known notes. But they date from May 2024, so they are already in the reported price/volume history (FY24–25 revenue per Gb). They are mid-throughput, not HT consumables, and list prices only. They show the pattern of deflation, not acceleration."}
